<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1081–1151</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Abbot Suger, Suger of Saint-Denis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suger was abbot of Saint-Denis near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from 1122 and an adviser to the French kings Louis VI and Louis VII, serving as regent while Louis VII was on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>. His rebuilding of the abbey church of Saint-Denis in the 1140s, with its pointed arches, rib vaults and large stained-glass windows, is usually regarded as the beginning of Gothic architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suger.jpg" alt="Suger" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uploaded by Hedning to Swedish Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbot_Suger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
